1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,480
I'm already so rich I don't need any more of a myself.

2
00:00:02,480 --> 00:00:05,320
I just want to help subscribers.

3
00:00:05,320 --> 00:00:06,200
It's my goal.

4
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:08,840
You just need to find two subscribers yourself.

5
00:00:08,840 --> 00:00:12,440
You just need to find two subscribers yourself.

6
00:00:12,440 --> 00:00:13,760
And then they find two people.

7
00:00:13,760 --> 00:00:15,040
And then they find two people.

8
00:00:15,040 --> 00:00:16,960
Like something might be like, look,

9
00:00:16,960 --> 00:00:18,520
I mean I only ask for $1,000 a month

10
00:00:18,520 --> 00:00:20,520
just so you take me seriously.

11
00:00:20,520 --> 00:00:22,880
I just want you to know that this is a serious matter.

12
00:00:22,880 --> 00:00:26,000
So I mean, it's just $1,000 just to get you paying attention.

13
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:28,360
And then, I don't need the money.

14
00:00:28,360 --> 00:00:30,920
Why would I?

15
00:00:30,920 --> 00:00:32,560
So anyway, so even strong critics,

16
00:00:32,560 --> 00:00:35,240
as long as they actually do math,

17
00:00:35,240 --> 00:00:38,680
will admit that it performs very well in a lot of ways.

18
00:00:38,680 --> 00:00:40,840
So it's not in the bin of theories

19
00:00:40,840 --> 00:00:44,320
that are completely wrong, whatever people might sound

20
00:00:44,320 --> 00:00:46,080
if they're just doing a sound bite.

21
00:00:46,080 --> 00:00:49,560
So for example, it is very common for asset prices

22
00:00:49,560 --> 00:00:53,240
to fall when firms announce good news.

23
00:00:53,240 --> 00:00:54,960
And the efficient markets hypothesis

24
00:00:54,960 --> 00:00:57,040
is a very clear explanation.

25
00:00:57,040 --> 00:01:01,440
Why would prices fall when I announce profits?

26
00:01:01,440 --> 00:01:04,600
Yes, because the market expected even better profits.

27
00:01:04,600 --> 00:01:08,360
Why can the price go up when you announce losses?

28
00:01:08,360 --> 00:01:11,120
Because the market expected worse losses.

29
00:01:11,120 --> 00:01:14,600
This makes no sense in terms of a naive,

30
00:01:14,600 --> 00:01:15,560
present-oriented model.

31
00:01:15,560 --> 00:01:17,200
It makes great sense in terms of the efficient markets

32
00:01:17,200 --> 00:01:18,400
hypothesis.

33
00:01:18,400 --> 00:01:20,920
And my dad has repeatedly complained to me

34
00:01:20,920 --> 00:01:23,160
about how good news came out, and the price went down.

35
00:01:23,160 --> 00:01:24,520
Explain that.

36
00:01:24,520 --> 00:01:28,800
Actually, it's a prediction of the standard model.

37
00:01:28,800 --> 00:01:31,960
And yet, it really bugs him.

38
00:01:31,960 --> 00:01:34,040
And now, anyway, so my dad is a very smart guy.

39
00:01:34,040 --> 00:01:35,840
So that's something where we tell him that.

40
00:01:35,840 --> 00:01:37,880
It's like, for that conversation, he's like, no,

41
00:01:37,880 --> 00:01:39,240
it's OK, I get it.

42
00:01:39,240 --> 00:01:41,120
But he doesn't retain it at all.

43
00:01:41,120 --> 00:01:43,560
He gets angry again the next time it occurs.

44
00:01:46,640 --> 00:01:48,440
And efficient markets hypothesis also

45
00:01:48,440 --> 00:01:51,400
passes some surprising empirical tests.

46
00:01:51,400 --> 00:01:54,600
For example, you cannot really predict annual returns

47
00:01:54,600 --> 00:01:58,640
for the S&P 500 using early annual returns.

48
00:01:58,640 --> 00:01:59,480
So I've tried this.

49
00:01:59,480 --> 00:02:01,400
So you just put them in.

50
00:02:01,400 --> 00:02:04,160
There is a standard view among a lot of investors

51
00:02:04,160 --> 00:02:08,800
that it's either a good year to invest or a bad year to invest.

52
00:02:08,800 --> 00:02:12,080
And the basic story seems to be, if there's

53
00:02:12,080 --> 00:02:13,840
been a few good years in a row, then this

54
00:02:13,840 --> 00:02:14,800
is going to be another good year.

55
00:02:14,800 --> 00:02:17,400
And a few bad years, it's going to be another bad year.

56
00:02:17,400 --> 00:02:18,600
There could be a turnaround.

57
00:02:18,600 --> 00:02:20,960
But normally, it's based upon just saying,

58
00:02:20,960 --> 00:02:22,760
well, things will keep going kind of the way

59
00:02:22,760 --> 00:02:27,160
they've been going in this sort of short to medium run.

60
00:02:27,160 --> 00:02:31,880
This is one of my dad's favorite ideas, is, is this a good year?

61
00:02:31,880 --> 00:02:33,880
So there are good years, there are bad years.

62
00:02:33,880 --> 00:02:37,160
And the data say there's just no such thing.

63
00:02:37,160 --> 00:02:39,840
Now, it's so easy to believe this, though,

64
00:02:39,840 --> 00:02:42,320
because any time you get two good or two good bad years

65
00:02:42,320 --> 00:02:45,920
in a row, the theory is confirmed.

66
00:02:45,920 --> 00:02:48,040
There's just a little problem.

67
00:02:48,040 --> 00:02:50,600
What about when you have a good year and then a bad year,

68
00:02:50,600 --> 00:02:52,720
a bad year and then a good year?

69
00:02:52,720 --> 00:02:55,840
That throws off the whole correlation, right?

70
00:02:55,840 --> 00:02:57,960
And again, the reason why we don't find anything

71
00:02:57,960 --> 00:03:00,800
in the regression is because those two,

72
00:03:00,800 --> 00:03:02,320
the patterns all bounce out.

73
00:03:02,320 --> 00:03:05,680
And so in the end, it's just a blob of dots.

74
00:03:05,680 --> 00:03:07,720
And yet, any time there's two good years in a row,

75
00:03:07,720 --> 00:03:09,200
there's people saying, see, I told you so.

76
00:03:09,200 --> 00:03:10,760
Last year was good, this year's good.

77
00:03:10,760 --> 00:03:12,320
That's the way the world works.

78
00:03:12,320 --> 00:03:16,400
Except when it doesn't, which is about half the time.

79
00:03:16,400 --> 00:03:16,920
All right.

80
00:03:16,920 --> 00:03:18,320
I mean, it wouldn't literally have

81
00:03:18,320 --> 00:03:21,080
happened if when there's a turnaround, it's very sharp.

82
00:03:21,080 --> 00:03:24,880
So it could actually be that 2 3rds of the time,

83
00:03:24,880 --> 00:03:26,800
like it went up last year, it goes up again.

84
00:03:26,800 --> 00:03:29,880
But if it splits from going up to going down, it crashes.

85
00:03:29,880 --> 00:03:32,960
Then again, that would give you no, like a zero regression

86
00:03:32,960 --> 00:03:35,240
coefficient.

87
00:03:35,240 --> 00:03:37,240
And no, there's a whole industry of specialists

88
00:03:37,240 --> 00:03:39,120
out there who do tell you whether it's

89
00:03:39,120 --> 00:03:40,920
a good year or a bad year between investing.

90
00:03:40,920 --> 00:03:44,160
And data say that they're just wrong.

91
00:03:44,160 --> 00:03:47,120
As to whether they are mistaken or lying

92
00:03:47,120 --> 00:03:49,280
is an interesting question.

93
00:03:49,280 --> 00:03:52,920
So they could just be highly innumerate.

94
00:03:52,920 --> 00:03:55,800
So what do you think about people who run astrology funds?

95
00:03:55,800 --> 00:03:57,520
There are real astrology funds out there

96
00:03:57,520 --> 00:03:59,880
who use astrology to break the stock market.

97
00:03:59,880 --> 00:04:03,480
So do you think that they know that they are making stuff up?

98
00:04:03,480 --> 00:04:06,160
Or do they drink their own Kool-Aid?

99
00:04:06,160 --> 00:04:07,880
Or a little bit of each?

100
00:04:07,880 --> 00:04:10,320
Sorry, I'm not a mind reader.

101
00:04:10,320 --> 00:04:12,640
I do have a lot of trouble believing someone

102
00:04:12,640 --> 00:04:14,640
could work in Wall Street and trade

103
00:04:14,640 --> 00:04:15,720
on the basis of astrology.

104
00:04:15,720 --> 00:04:19,080
But that could just be a failure of my imagination,

105
00:04:19,080 --> 00:04:22,480
lack of empathy for other people.

106
00:04:22,480 --> 00:04:24,480
And the Efficient Markets Hypothesis

107
00:04:24,480 --> 00:04:28,200
has had great real world success in explaining

108
00:04:28,200 --> 00:04:32,480
the rise of the index funds, which

109
00:04:32,480 --> 00:04:34,600
is one of the most counterintuitive investment

110
00:04:34,600 --> 00:04:36,920
opportunities in the world.

111
00:04:36,920 --> 00:04:38,840
The idea of an index fund is we're

112
00:04:38,840 --> 00:04:41,320
going to stop trying to beat the market.

113
00:04:41,320 --> 00:04:43,720
We're going to stop trying to succeed.

114
00:04:43,720 --> 00:04:45,680
We're giving up on success.

115
00:04:45,680 --> 00:04:50,120
Instead, what we're going to do is just buy one share each

116
00:04:50,120 --> 00:04:54,480
of everything in the S&P 500 and sit on it until we retire.

117
00:04:54,480 --> 00:04:55,840
Until you want to sell.

118
00:04:55,840 --> 00:04:56,920
That's the plan.

119
00:04:56,920 --> 00:04:58,600
That's the whole idea of the index fund.

120
00:04:58,600 --> 00:05:01,440
Just go and buy whatever the S&P 500 is,

121
00:05:01,440 --> 00:05:03,080
or buy whatever some other index is.

122
00:05:03,080 --> 00:05:08,720
Buy the Dow Jones, or buy the FTSE, or buy the DAX.

123
00:05:08,720 --> 00:05:11,840
Just buy that and hold it.

124
00:05:11,840 --> 00:05:13,840
And then how will you beat the market?

125
00:05:13,840 --> 00:05:14,920
We won't.

126
00:05:14,920 --> 00:05:15,800
We're not trying to.

127
00:05:15,800 --> 00:05:17,400
We've given up on that.

128
00:05:17,400 --> 00:05:20,400
It's like, well, why should I give you my money then?

129
00:05:20,400 --> 00:05:25,120
Because you will do better than if you tried.

130
00:05:25,120 --> 00:05:25,640
Right?

131
00:05:25,640 --> 00:05:27,960
And boy, how can I do better?

132
00:05:27,960 --> 00:05:30,720
How can I do worse if I try?

133
00:05:30,720 --> 00:05:33,440
It's like, well, let's just do an experiment.

134
00:05:33,440 --> 00:05:35,960
All right, you go try, and I won't.

135
00:05:35,960 --> 00:05:38,560
And we'll see who has more money at the end.

136
00:05:38,560 --> 00:05:43,400
And a lot of what's made index funds so successful

137
00:05:43,400 --> 00:05:45,680
is just this demonstration.

138
00:05:45,680 --> 00:05:48,320
Look, I'm going to buy it, and I'm going to beat you.

139
00:05:48,320 --> 00:05:50,640
And you're going to be like, but I can hire the smartest

140
00:05:50,640 --> 00:05:51,480
people in the world.

141
00:05:51,480 --> 00:05:52,400
You're welcome to try.

142
00:05:52,400 --> 00:05:54,200
Hire them all, for all I care.

143
00:05:54,200 --> 00:05:55,680
I still think I'm going to beat you.

144
00:05:55,680 --> 00:05:58,120
Now, an obvious reason for this would

145
00:05:58,120 --> 00:06:02,720
be that you just burn up so much in the cost of hiring experts

146
00:06:02,720 --> 00:06:04,680
that it just doesn't like that you just wind up

147
00:06:04,680 --> 00:06:06,280
once not being worth it.

148
00:06:06,280 --> 00:06:07,880
Although actually, at least in the US,

149
00:06:07,880 --> 00:06:09,840
index funds have generally done better,

150
00:06:09,840 --> 00:06:11,680
even on the gross return.

151
00:06:11,680 --> 00:06:13,560
So never mind net, where you subtract out

152
00:06:13,560 --> 00:06:16,600
the transactions costs or the fees.

153
00:06:16,600 --> 00:06:20,040
Even on gross, it's very common for the S&P 500

154
00:06:20,040 --> 00:06:25,600
to beat the gross of actively traded funds, which certainly

155
00:06:25,600 --> 00:06:27,680
just seems to suggest that the active traders,

156
00:06:27,680 --> 00:06:29,160
like they know less than nothing.

157
00:06:29,160 --> 00:06:31,600
They actually have reversed their buoyance.

158
00:06:31,600 --> 00:06:33,440
Well, actually, they put in a lot of effort,

159
00:06:33,440 --> 00:06:35,800
and they managed to do something worse than just the most

160
00:06:35,800 --> 00:06:36,360
obvious thing.

161
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:40,440
So again, this is counterintuitive,

162
00:06:40,440 --> 00:06:43,600
because normally we think that you will do better if you try.

163
00:06:43,600 --> 00:06:45,140
And again, this is another thing that

164
00:06:45,140 --> 00:06:48,720
gets my dad very, very annoyed, is that he actually

165
00:06:48,720 --> 00:06:50,160
does own index funds, and he knows

166
00:06:50,160 --> 00:06:50,880
they're beating the market.

167
00:06:50,880 --> 00:06:52,080
How can they beat the market?

168
00:06:52,080 --> 00:06:54,160
They're not even trying.

169
00:06:54,160 --> 00:06:56,000
And when I tell him, look, there's actually

170
00:06:56,000 --> 00:06:56,920
good reason for it.

171
00:06:56,920 --> 00:06:59,600
And then he'll understand it for a couple minutes,

172
00:06:59,600 --> 00:07:02,840
but then next time I talk to him, he's angry again.

173
00:07:02,840 --> 00:07:04,960
Why would you be angry that you're doing something

174
00:07:04,960 --> 00:07:08,200
that is really easy and does better than people trying?

175
00:07:08,200 --> 00:07:09,720
I don't know.

176
00:07:09,720 --> 00:07:12,560
There's some kind of violation of the Puritan work ethic,

177
00:07:12,560 --> 00:07:17,120
that you can beat the market by making no effort at all.

178
00:07:17,120 --> 00:07:20,040
So anyway, Fish and Market's Analysis predicts this,

179
00:07:20,040 --> 00:07:24,360
because it says that since the market is not predictable,

180
00:07:24,360 --> 00:07:27,680
if you simply buy and hold a diverse portfolio,

181
00:07:27,680 --> 00:07:29,800
you will do as well as anyone else,

182
00:07:29,800 --> 00:07:31,400
but you'll save on transactions costs,

183
00:07:31,400 --> 00:07:32,760
and so you'll actually do better.

184
00:07:35,040 --> 00:07:38,080
And let's see, are there, say, probably more than one paper.

185
00:07:38,080 --> 00:07:40,480
There's one paper a few years ago showing

186
00:07:40,480 --> 00:07:44,080
that at least holding general investment strategy constant,

187
00:07:44,080 --> 00:07:47,360
women do better than men, because women trade less.

188
00:07:47,360 --> 00:07:49,920
Men are more likely to get the idea they know something

189
00:07:49,920 --> 00:07:51,920
and then buy it by herself.

190
00:07:51,920 --> 00:07:53,920
And then on average, they're wrong,

191
00:07:53,920 --> 00:07:55,800
but they still lose the trading fees.

192
00:08:02,720 --> 00:08:05,000
And the origin of these index funds,

193
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:07,680
so it does actually come out of academia,

194
00:08:07,680 --> 00:08:10,840
where they, so let's see,

195
00:08:10,840 --> 00:08:13,920
Princeton professor that I TA for, Bert Malkiel,

196
00:08:13,920 --> 00:08:16,640
teamed up with an investment guy named John Boggle,

197
00:08:16,640 --> 00:08:20,320
and they were both instrumental in Vanguard.

198
00:08:20,320 --> 00:08:23,160
Not quite sure how, Malkiel at least was close

199
00:08:23,160 --> 00:08:24,600
to the ground floor.

200
00:08:24,600 --> 00:08:26,040
So Malkiel is mostly best known

201
00:08:26,040 --> 00:08:28,240
for his random walk down Wall Street book.

202
00:08:28,240 --> 00:08:30,000
But anyway, it's the idea of, well,

203
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:33,880
given that the market follows a random walk very closely,

204
00:08:33,880 --> 00:08:37,600
why bother going and hiring any experts to go and pick stocks

205
00:08:37,600 --> 00:08:42,000
when you can just go and buy and hold a simple index fund?

206
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:44,680
Right, and again, the idea was so counterintuitive at first

207
00:08:44,680 --> 00:08:47,080
that they only, they started off with,

208
00:08:47,080 --> 00:08:48,600
of course, 0% of the market.

209
00:08:48,600 --> 00:08:51,040
It took them years even to get to like 5%.

210
00:08:51,040 --> 00:08:54,040
I think now they're up to like 40, 45% of the market.

211
00:08:54,040 --> 00:08:55,800
And of course, people who hold these funds,

212
00:08:55,800 --> 00:08:58,560
probably most of them have no idea why it works.

213
00:08:58,560 --> 00:09:02,080
All they know is it's fairly simple and other people do it,

214
00:09:02,080 --> 00:09:04,000
and it does as well as the market, or better,

215
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:08,160
and the transaction fees are super low.

216
00:09:08,160 --> 00:09:10,360
And while you're on the transaction fees being super

217
00:09:10,360 --> 00:09:12,160
low, know that Vanguard is way, way lower

218
00:09:12,160 --> 00:09:14,520
than Teacraft for exactly the same fund.

219
00:09:14,520 --> 00:09:18,240
So George Mason bizarrely lets you use Vanguard

220
00:09:18,240 --> 00:09:20,920
for some things, or he lets you choose between Vanguard

221
00:09:20,920 --> 00:09:22,680
and Teacraft for some things, but you have

222
00:09:22,680 --> 00:09:25,040
to do Teacraft for the others.

223
00:09:25,040 --> 00:09:26,920
So this is a small difference.

224
00:09:26,920 --> 00:09:30,600
For Vanguard, the fee is like 0.02%,

225
00:09:30,600 --> 00:09:33,360
and for Teacraft, it's like 0.2%.

226
00:09:33,360 --> 00:09:35,320
So by that, it's 10 times the difference,

227
00:09:35,320 --> 00:09:38,600
and we're doing exactly the same thing.

228
00:09:38,600 --> 00:09:39,960
All right.

229
00:09:39,960 --> 00:09:42,120
And then as I said, when you look at net returns,

230
00:09:42,120 --> 00:09:45,520
that is even more weird that index funds are not going,

231
00:09:45,520 --> 00:09:47,200
are not even bigger than they are.

232
00:09:47,200 --> 00:09:48,360
Because the asserts theory suggests

233
00:09:48,360 --> 00:09:50,520
that it should be the net returns that equalize,

234
00:09:50,520 --> 00:09:52,880
but so far, it looks more like the gross returns

235
00:09:52,880 --> 00:09:54,560
that equalize.

236
00:09:54,560 --> 00:09:56,640
Right now, many people pointed out quite properly

237
00:09:56,640 --> 00:10:00,520
that if everyone did indexing, it wouldn't work anymore.

238
00:10:00,520 --> 00:10:03,400
But we haven't gotten there yet.

239
00:10:03,400 --> 00:10:05,000
Indexing had been around for 50 years,

240
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:10,200
and we're still slowly moving towards final equilibrium.

241
00:10:10,200 --> 00:10:13,040
Like once it was 99% of people indexing,

242
00:10:13,040 --> 00:10:15,240
then the few people that were around who would say,

243
00:10:15,240 --> 00:10:16,400
you know what?

244
00:10:16,400 --> 00:10:17,840
The hurricane's coming to Florida,

245
00:10:17,840 --> 00:10:22,120
so I'm going to go and buy orange futures, which

