00:10:45,480 --> 00:10:46,880
All right.

260
00:10:46,880 --> 00:10:51,040
So despite the logic of the AMH, there

261
00:10:51,040 --> 00:10:53,680
are some noted anomalies.

262
00:10:53,680 --> 00:10:55,120
All right.

263
00:10:55,120 --> 00:10:59,000
Some of the best publicized are so-called calendar effects.

264
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:07,320
And the best known one is the January effect, which

265
00:11:07,320 --> 00:11:08,880
at least used to be super solid.

266
00:11:08,880 --> 00:11:13,840
Now, the least in common view is the January effect

267
00:11:13,840 --> 00:11:14,760
has finally gone away.

268
00:11:14,760 --> 00:11:16,320
But it still lasted for decades.

269
00:11:16,320 --> 00:11:19,240
So it's still worth talking about, even if it's gone

270
00:11:19,240 --> 00:11:21,440
and doesn't help you anymore.

271
00:11:21,440 --> 00:11:23,680
So Thaler goes over the evidence at the time.

272
00:11:23,680 --> 00:11:24,440
Yeah, Jonathan?

273
00:11:24,440 --> 00:11:26,520
Do you think part of the reason that it went away

274
00:11:26,520 --> 00:11:28,360
is that this was published 35 years ago now?

275
00:11:28,360 --> 00:11:28,760
Yeah.

276
00:11:28,760 --> 00:11:29,260
Yeah, yeah.

277
00:11:29,260 --> 00:11:31,200
So something that people would say,

278
00:11:31,200 --> 00:11:33,720
the industry would sometimes say is now the January effect's

279
00:11:33,720 --> 00:11:36,560
happening in December or November.

280
00:11:36,560 --> 00:11:39,400
And then eventually, it's like, well, we can't find it anymore.

281
00:11:39,400 --> 00:11:42,600
But yeah, so that's pretty simple.

282
00:11:42,600 --> 00:11:43,080
All right.

283
00:11:43,080 --> 00:11:46,280
So anyway, so for the period that Richard Thaler

284
00:11:46,280 --> 00:11:49,240
is talking about, so you've got an average New York Stock

285
00:11:49,240 --> 00:11:51,920
Exchange monthly returns from February, December, 0.5%

286
00:11:51,920 --> 00:11:54,880
per month, and for January, 3.5.

287
00:11:54,880 --> 00:11:56,840
So seven times the average for one month.

288
00:11:56,840 --> 00:11:58,360
That seems pretty good.

289
00:11:58,360 --> 00:12:01,640
And looking more closely, seems like it's primarily

290
00:12:01,640 --> 00:12:06,040
just from really high returns for small firms during January.

291
00:12:06,040 --> 00:12:10,480
So it's a subset of the one month, one subset of firms

292
00:12:10,480 --> 00:12:13,080
driving this enormous return.

293
00:12:13,080 --> 00:12:15,520
And if it were just one country, it'd be easy to dismiss.

294
00:12:15,520 --> 00:12:17,840
But so at least what Thaler's writing,

295
00:12:17,840 --> 00:12:21,200
January effect's found in 15 out of 16 countries studied.

296
00:12:21,200 --> 00:12:24,600
So again, that's pretty impressive.

297
00:12:24,600 --> 00:12:26,920
So that's the big one that's been talked about.

298
00:12:26,920 --> 00:12:29,840
And again, now, it may no longer exist.

299
00:12:29,840 --> 00:12:32,840
But the fact that it was around for decades still gives one

300
00:12:32,840 --> 00:12:33,560
pause.

301
00:12:33,560 --> 00:12:36,280
What's the explanation for it?

302
00:12:36,280 --> 00:12:38,240
So good question.

303
00:12:38,240 --> 00:12:40,160
So if you read Thaler, and I believe

304
00:12:40,160 --> 00:12:43,800
the chapter is assigned for the final exam and everything,

305
00:12:43,800 --> 00:12:47,200
one popular story is something to do with tax law.

306
00:12:47,200 --> 00:12:51,600
And a lot of people just want to go and sell stuff

307
00:12:51,600 --> 00:12:55,640
right before, either right before or right after.

308
00:12:55,640 --> 00:12:57,440
But again, I think what he said is actually

309
00:12:57,440 --> 00:13:00,120
if you go and look at countries where they do or don't

310
00:13:00,120 --> 00:13:02,200
have these tax incentives, you didn't actually

311
00:13:02,200 --> 00:13:03,800
see much of a difference.

312
00:13:03,800 --> 00:13:05,240
So that's wrong.

313
00:13:05,240 --> 00:13:08,240
So then it's pretty hard to know.

314
00:13:08,240 --> 00:13:10,480
My memory is Thaler doesn't have any good story.

315
00:13:10,480 --> 00:13:12,080
It's like, well, you could either

316
00:13:12,080 --> 00:13:14,080
sit around saying I won't believe it until you

317
00:13:14,080 --> 00:13:16,580
have a good story, or you could just make some money going

318
00:13:16,580 --> 00:13:17,760
and buying in January.

319
00:13:17,760 --> 00:13:22,200
And it worked pretty well for a couple decades.

320
00:13:22,200 --> 00:13:23,320
All right.

321
00:13:23,320 --> 00:13:31,320
Now another calendar effect, which again is not as

322
00:13:31,320 --> 00:13:33,520
devastating and harder to actually make money on it,

323
00:13:33,520 --> 00:13:35,040
but it's called the weekend effect.

324
00:13:35,040 --> 00:13:38,880
So when you go and leave money in the bank

325
00:13:38,880 --> 00:13:41,880
over the weekend for three days, how many days worth of interest

326
00:13:41,880 --> 00:13:42,840
do you earn?

327
00:13:42,840 --> 00:13:43,360
Three.

328
00:13:43,360 --> 00:13:44,600
Three days.

329
00:13:44,600 --> 00:13:48,600
When you go and when the stock market closes over the weekend,

330
00:13:48,600 --> 00:13:50,960
how much of an extra return do you get on Monday

331
00:13:50,960 --> 00:13:53,160
when it opens up?

332
00:13:53,160 --> 00:13:55,520
It seems like you only get one day instead of three days.

333
00:13:55,520 --> 00:13:57,320
And that seems weird.

334
00:13:57,320 --> 00:13:59,800
So if you hold debt, you get three days worth of interest

335
00:13:59,800 --> 00:14:00,960
over the weekend.

336
00:14:00,960 --> 00:14:04,200
So why don't you get the same for stocks?

337
00:14:04,200 --> 00:14:06,520
But anyway, Monday returns don't seem especially high.

338
00:14:06,520 --> 00:14:07,520
And again, that's weird.

339
00:14:07,520 --> 00:14:10,600
You think there'd be arbitrage, like why not go and dump all?

340
00:14:10,600 --> 00:14:15,320
So since debt pays triple over the weekend and stocks don't,

341
00:14:15,320 --> 00:14:17,600
why not dump all your stocks right

342
00:14:17,600 --> 00:14:19,760
before the end of the day Friday, put it all in bonds.

343
00:14:19,760 --> 00:14:22,220
And then every morning, you get out of that extra interest,

344
00:14:22,220 --> 00:14:23,480
buy stocks.

345
00:14:23,480 --> 00:14:25,820
All right, now there is the problem of transactions costs,

346
00:14:25,820 --> 00:14:27,680
which Thaler is happy to admit.

347
00:14:27,680 --> 00:14:29,440
Even there, though, they're at the margin.

348
00:14:29,440 --> 00:14:33,600
If you were planning on selling, why not arrange all your selling

349
00:14:33,600 --> 00:14:35,520
to be at the end of the day of stocks,

350
00:14:35,520 --> 00:14:37,560
sell all at the end of the day on February,

351
00:14:37,560 --> 00:14:41,560
arrange all of your buying to be at the opening on Monday?

352
00:14:41,560 --> 00:14:44,960
So that would seem like a good idea.

353
00:14:44,960 --> 00:14:49,000
So Thaler, being one of the reasonable critics of EMH,

354
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:52,160
is willing to admit cases where transactions costs mess up

355
00:14:52,160 --> 00:14:54,200
your opportunity to make money.

356
00:14:54,200 --> 00:14:55,720
All right, but again, like he says,

357
00:14:55,720 --> 00:14:59,240
well, look, still, we should see people going and timing

358
00:14:59,240 --> 00:15:02,720
all of their sales and purchases around these things.

359
00:15:02,720 --> 00:15:05,240
So basically, if you know that you're going to sell,

360
00:15:05,240 --> 00:15:07,880
why not sell at the minute where it's going to be best for you?

361
00:15:07,880 --> 00:15:09,160
If you know where you're going to buy,

362
00:15:09,160 --> 00:15:11,660
why not buy at the minutes that are going to be best for you?

363
00:15:11,660 --> 00:15:14,880
And yet, it seems like very few people actually do this.

364
00:15:18,720 --> 00:15:20,440
All right, so let's see.

365
00:15:20,440 --> 00:15:24,920
And then there's actually a series of papers on,

366
00:15:24,920 --> 00:15:26,080
what is it?

367
00:15:26,080 --> 00:15:29,000
So stock markets and seasonal affective disorder.

368
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:31,080
So at least there's a paper in the ER

369
00:15:31,080 --> 00:15:34,640
saying that you can actually see that stock markets

370
00:15:34,640 --> 00:15:40,040
around the world, so basically, like, so let's see.

371
00:15:40,040 --> 00:15:44,880
So basically, stock prices go up in the northern hemisphere

372
00:15:44,880 --> 00:15:49,640
during the summer and go down during May through July

373
00:15:49,640 --> 00:15:51,480
and go down in the southern hemisphere.

374
00:15:51,480 --> 00:15:53,060
And actually claiming that even if you

375
00:15:53,060 --> 00:15:54,720
look at the city of the stock market,

376
00:15:54,720 --> 00:15:56,480
that the amount of sunlight they're getting

377
00:15:56,480 --> 00:16:00,520
seems to be affecting the price, which on the one hand,

378
00:16:00,520 --> 00:16:02,600
is a little hard to believe because you think,

379
00:16:02,600 --> 00:16:04,760
like, arbitrage should handle this.

380
00:16:04,760 --> 00:16:07,720
But the pattern is actually pretty interesting.

381
00:16:07,720 --> 00:16:09,440
I did meet one of the authors, and she

382
00:16:09,440 --> 00:16:12,640
seemed to have a lot of details.

383
00:16:12,640 --> 00:16:14,760
I haven't talked to anyone who disagrees with you,

384
00:16:14,760 --> 00:16:16,880
but you've got a pretty good story anyway.

385
00:16:16,880 --> 00:16:19,880
I seem to pass a bunch of surprising tests, like the

386
00:16:19,880 --> 00:16:22,520
closer you are to the equator, the lower this is,

387
00:16:22,520 --> 00:16:24,720
because then the less of a change in daylight

388
00:16:24,720 --> 00:16:25,440
you're getting.

389
00:16:25,440 --> 00:16:26,900
Because seasonal affective disorder

390
00:16:26,900 --> 00:16:29,360
is all about people getting depressed by too much night.

391
00:16:29,360 --> 00:16:30,900
Could be too depressed to arbitrage.

392
00:16:30,900 --> 00:16:32,280
Yes, too depressed to arbitrage.

393
00:16:32,280 --> 00:16:36,000
That astrology thing wasn't so crazy after all.

394
00:16:36,000 --> 00:16:38,960
Well, the constant problem with astrology

395
00:16:38,960 --> 00:16:41,320
is there isn't even a standard astrological prediction.

396
00:16:41,320 --> 00:16:43,360
Seasonal affective disorder does have predictions

397
00:16:43,360 --> 00:16:46,240
that are specific and so testable.

398
00:16:46,240 --> 00:16:49,520
All right, now we come to one of my favorite ones,

399
00:16:49,520 --> 00:16:50,960
a mean reversion.

400
00:16:50,960 --> 00:16:54,480
So Fisher-Marcus hypothesis says that returns are unpredictable.

401
00:16:54,480 --> 00:16:57,320
You can't use past returns to forecast future.

402
00:16:57,320 --> 00:17:00,000
But there is a literature on mean reversion

403
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:02,960
saying that that is too hasty, and at least

404
00:17:02,960 --> 00:17:05,800
for certain assets.

405
00:17:05,800 --> 00:17:08,160
The fact that rates have been the thing,

406
00:17:08,160 --> 00:17:10,240
the market's been going up a lot,

407
00:17:10,240 --> 00:17:12,400
means that you should predict it will go down.

408
00:17:12,400 --> 00:17:13,780
And the fact it's been going down

409
00:17:13,780 --> 00:17:15,200
means it should go up.

410
00:17:15,200 --> 00:17:17,080
Which, notice, is basically the exact opposite

411
00:17:17,080 --> 00:17:18,400
of the normal theory.

412
00:17:18,400 --> 00:17:20,920
The normal theory is like momentum.

413
00:17:20,920 --> 00:17:23,240
Good things follow good, bad things follow bad.

414
00:17:23,240 --> 00:17:25,760
Mean reversion is the opposite.

415
00:17:25,760 --> 00:17:28,360
It says that you know how everything's been going great?

416
00:17:28,360 --> 00:17:29,880
Expect bad stuff to happen.

417
00:17:29,880 --> 00:17:31,680
You know how everything's been going poorly?

418
00:17:31,680 --> 00:17:34,160
Expect good things to happen.

419
00:17:34,160 --> 00:17:35,600
All right, yes?

420
00:17:35,600 --> 00:17:39,120
Just sort of clarify, though, with mean reversion,

421
00:17:39,120 --> 00:17:42,040
would it then, like if something goes up by a certain amount,

422
00:17:42,040 --> 00:17:44,400
does it have to drop by that same exact amount?

423
00:17:44,400 --> 00:17:45,600
Or just a significant amount?

424
00:17:45,600 --> 00:17:46,100
Right.

425
00:17:46,100 --> 00:17:48,260
Again, the way that you normally do it statistically

426
00:17:48,260 --> 00:17:51,400
is just to see whether there is a statistically significant

427
00:17:51,400 --> 00:17:53,120
tendency for higher average returns

428
00:17:53,120 --> 00:17:56,640
in the past to actually have a negative sign.

429
00:17:56,640 --> 00:17:59,880
But yeah, so it doesn't actually require that you

430
00:17:59,880 --> 00:18:02,800
go back to a specific mean.

431
00:18:02,800 --> 00:18:05,280
It just means that when you're above a historic mean,

432
00:18:05,280 --> 00:18:06,880
you tend to go down.

433
00:18:06,880 --> 00:18:09,600
And but again, like tendency can be quite slow.

434
00:18:09,600 --> 00:18:13,400
So mean reversion is one of my favorites.

435
00:18:13,400 --> 00:18:18,720
So when I was betting Tyler Cowen on the price of gasoline,

436
00:18:18,720 --> 00:18:20,160
mean reversion was in my mind.

437
00:18:20,160 --> 00:18:21,360
That's all I knew.

438
00:18:21,360 --> 00:18:24,360
So I talked to James Hamilton, by many accounts,

439
00:18:24,360 --> 00:18:26,880
the best empirical energy economist in the world.

440
00:18:26,880 --> 00:18:29,680
And he totally believed in mean reversion.

441
00:18:29,680 --> 00:18:32,200
And the argument got started because Tyler Cowen said

442
00:18:32,200 --> 00:18:34,400
Julian Simon didn't know what he was talking about,

443
00:18:34,400 --> 00:18:36,960
because the EMH rules out the idea

444
00:18:36,960 --> 00:18:39,800
that natural resource prices tend to go back down

445
00:18:39,800 --> 00:18:40,840
when they're high.

446
00:18:40,840 --> 00:18:43,840
And I said, you know, Simon had 150 years of data on his side,

447
00:18:43,840 --> 00:18:45,320
and you've got a theory.

448
00:18:45,320 --> 00:18:48,560
So I'm going to go 150 years worth of data.

449
00:18:48,560 --> 00:18:53,000
And yeah, so 10 years ago, when the price of gas

450
00:18:53,000 --> 00:18:54,840
was really high historically, that's

451
00:18:54,840 --> 00:18:57,680
when I went and bet that it would fall a lot.

452
00:18:57,680 --> 00:18:59,160
And I won.

453
00:18:59,160 --> 00:19:01,080
So of course, Tyler will say, like, this

454
00:19:01,080 --> 00:19:02,040
doesn't prove anything.

455
00:19:02,040 --> 00:19:03,120
I just got lucky.

456
00:19:03,120 --> 00:19:03,620
Right?

457
00:19:03,620 --> 00:19:06,600
But I mean, that really doesn't prove anything.

458
00:19:06,600 --> 00:19:10,600
But that you would put no weight on it all seems kind of odd.

459
00:19:10,600 --> 00:19:11,100
Right?

460
00:19:11,100 --> 00:19:13,640
Again, especially when the actual,

461
00:19:13,640 --> 00:19:15,160
like the standard view among people

462
00:19:15,160 --> 00:19:18,080
know the data is gas is like petroleum in general

463
00:19:18,080 --> 00:19:20,720
is mean reversion.

464
00:19:20,720 --> 00:19:23,280
So again, as to why that might be,

465
00:19:23,280 --> 00:19:25,240
so again, like we're talking about commodities.

466
00:19:25,240 --> 00:19:28,480
The storage cost alone could give you mean reversion, right?

467
00:19:28,480 --> 00:19:32,960
So again, like suppose that the price of something

468
00:19:32,960 --> 00:19:35,440
is really high.

469
00:19:35,440 --> 00:19:35,960
Right?

470
00:19:35,960 --> 00:19:38,280
You might say, well, it's not possible it's going to go down,

471
00:19:38,280 --> 00:19:39,440
because people knew it was going to go down.

472
00:19:39,440 --> 00:19:41,160
And they would just sell off their whole stockpile.

473
00:19:41,160 --> 00:19:44,320
And that would drive prices back down to the normal level.

474
00:19:44,320 --> 00:19:44,820
All right?

475
00:19:44,820 --> 00:19:48,480
But again, what if it's actually a big problem

476
00:19:48,480 --> 00:19:50,600
to go and sell it off quickly?

477
00:19:50,600 --> 00:19:53,240
For example, like to sell off petroleum really quickly

478
00:19:53,240 --> 00:19:54,960
right now, suppose you knew that, like,

479
00:19:54,960 --> 00:19:56,400
suppose you owned an oil well.

480
00:19:56,400 --> 00:19:58,360
And you knew that next year the price of oil

481
00:19:58,360 --> 00:20:10,920
would fall by 90%?


